      "notes": "Chapter 50's computer on one bus. Masters: the RV32I system core (boot ROM: the ROM part on its left) and the DMA engine. Slaves, by the top four address bits: 0 RAM (16 KB), 1 the flash (a 64-cycle page read, then 2 cycles a word; its program is the ROM part beside it: Edit program…), 2 the DRAM (chapter 48's controller), 4 the DMA's registers, 5 the screen (pixel (x, y) at 0x5000_0000 + 256·y + 4·x, colour the low byte), 6 the I/O port: the keyboard (+0 the oldest key, taken when read; +4 status) and the timer (+8 its count, reading it acknowledges; +12 its irq); anything else the default slave. Console, LEDs, switches and mtime are inside the core at 0x8000_0000. At power-on the boot ROM has the DMA copy the flash to RAM 0x400 and jumps there. Set the clock rate to max (the menu by Step), Run, then type (the keyboard takes keys while running): each key is echoed, kept in DRAM and drawn; Enter prints the line back; the LEDs count the timer's interrupts. Every link is two buses: the request (the master's nine signals, 105 bits) and the response (six signals, 37 bits); double-click a part to see them unpacked. The CPU drawer does not follow this core (its memory and interrupts are behind the bus): watch the console, the screen and the LEDs, or look inside."
