      "notes": "Chapter 67's switch with two of its four ports plugged in. Each plugged port is a MAC receiver (the bits of the computer's transmitter become the byte stream the switch takes) and a MAC transmitter (the switch's byte stream becomes bits for the computer's receiver); the receivers accept every destination (promiscuous), as a switch port must. Ports 2 and 3 are unplugged: nothing arrives, and their transmit sides are always ready, so a flooded frame leaves. Select the switch to read its learned table and counters."
