      "notes": "Two computers and a switch, as chapter 68 ends. Each computer (double-click to open it) is an RV32I bus core with a boot ROM, a 16 KB RAM and a NIC at 0x7000_0000 (chapter 68's: eight registers, two rings of descriptors in RAM, DMA as the interconnect's second master, a MAC inside), on a bus of its own. A is 02:00:00:00:00:0a and B :0b. A sends B a raw 60-byte Ethernet frame saying ping and prints B's pong, 3 times; B prints each frame it receives and answers. Open the switch (double-click): a MAC RX and a MAC TX a port join the computers' serial pairs (one bit every four clocks) to the byte streams of chapter 67's four-port learning switch; ports 2 and 3 are unplugged. Select the switch part inside to read its learned table and per-port counters live. The first ping is flooded (the switch has not met B yet), the pong goes to port 0 alone, and A's later pings go to port 1 alone: learned 2, flooded 1. A frame crosses the wire in about 2 300 clocks and the switch stores it before forwarding, so a ping and its pong take about 10 000: Run at the maximum clock rate and wait. The CPU drawer does not follow these cores (their memory is behind a bus): watch the consoles, or select a NIC and read its registers.",
